  "main_text": "<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is Yusuf Li with zip code 91148, and they want to change their LA order to their NYC address and exchange a Bluetooth Speaker for the cheapest green type. First, I need to authenticate the user, which was done using the find_user_id_by_name_zip function, giving user ID yusuf_li_7255.\n\nNext, I should get the user's details to find their orders and addresses. Using get_user_details with the user ID will provide their current orders and default address. But the user mentioned they have another order with the NYC address, so I need to check all their orders.\n\nOnce I have the list of orders, I need to identify the LA order (probably the one with the current default address) and the NYC order to get the correct address details. Then, modify the shipping address of the LA order to the NYC address using modify_pending_order_address. But wait, the order has to be pending to modify. So I need to check the status of the LA order using get_order_details. If it's pending, proceed; otherwise, can't modify.\n\nThen, for the Bluetooth Speaker exchange, the user wants the cheapest green type. I need to find the product ID of the Bluetooth Speaker, get its variants to find the cheapest green option. Then, check if the current item in the order can be exchanged. Since it's a delivered order, use exchange_delivered_order_items. But again, need to confirm the order status is delivered. Also, ensure the new item is of the same product type but different options. Calculate the price difference and confirm payment method.\n\nThroughout, I need to make sure each step is confirmed by the user, as per policy. Also, handle each request step by step, one tool call at a time. Let's start by getting the user's details to proceed.\n</think>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"get_user_details\", \"arguments\": {\"user_id\": \"yusuf_li_7255\"}}\n",
